.TH "WAY\-DISPLAYS" "5" "2026/08/12" "way\-displays" "File Formats Manual"
.SH NAME
way\-displays \- \f[B]\f[CB]cfg.yaml\f[B]\f[R] configuration file format.
.SH SYNTAX
way\-displays\(cq configuration file uses the YAML syntax with the schema:
.IP
.EX
https://raw.githubusercontent.com/alex\-courtis/way\-displays/refs/heads/master/schema/cfg\-2.0.0.yaml
.EE
.SH LOCATION
Configuration is read from \f[B]\f[CB]cfg.yaml\f[B]\f[R], discovered in order:
.IP
.EX
$XDG_CONFIG_HOME/way\-displays/cfg.yaml
$HOME/.config/way\-displays/cfg.yaml
/usr/local/etc/way\-displays/cfg.yaml
/etc/way\-displays/cfg.yaml
.EE
.PP
A default configuration file will be created if none is found.
.SH NAMING CONVENTIONS
<\f[I]name\f[R]> is matched by display name or description with precedence: exact, regex, fuzzy
.PP
You can identify them via logs or \f[B]\f[CB]way\-displays \-\-get\f[B]\f[R] or \f[B]\f[CB]way\-displays \-\-list\f[B]\f[R] e.g.
.IP
.EX
DP\-3 Arrived:
    name:     \(aqDP\-3\(aq
    desc:     \(aqUnknown Monitor Maker ABC123 (DP\-3 via HDMI)\(aq
.EE
.PP
It is recommended to use the description rather than the nondeterministic name.
.PP
Any item prefixed with a \f[B]\f[CB]!\f[B]\f[R] will be interpreted as extended POSIX regex e.g.\ \f[B]\f[CB]\(aq!\(haDP\-1\(aq\f[B]\f[R].
Regex strings must be single quoted.
.PP
Using a regex is preferred, however fuzzy case insensitive string matches of at least 4 characters may be used.
.PP
\f[CR]\(aqDP\-1\(aq\f[R] will match \f[CR]eDP\-1\f[R] and \f[CR]DP\-1\f[R].
Consider using regex \f[CR]\(aq!\(haDP\-1$\(aq\f[R] to exactly match.
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]ARRANGE, ALIGN\f[B]\f[R]
The default is to arrange in a row, aligned at the top of the displays.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]ARRANGE\f[B]\f[R]
Enum <\f[B]\f[CB]ROW\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]COLUMN\f[B]\f[R]>, default \f[B]\f[CB]ROW\f[B]\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]ALIGN\f[B]\f[R]
Enum <\f[B]\f[CB]TOP\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]MIDDLE\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]BOTTOM\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]LEFT\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]RIGHT\f[B]\f[R]>, default \f[B]\f[CB]TOP\f[B]\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]ALIGN\f[B]\f[R] (ROW)
<\f[B]\f[CB]TOP\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]MIDDLE\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]BOTTOM\f[B]\f[R]>
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]ALIGN\f[B]\f[R] (COLUMN)
<\f[B]\f[CB]LEFT\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]MIDDLE\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]RIGHT\f[B]\f[R]>
.PP
Layout to suit you e.g.\ top to bottom, aligned in the centre:
.IP
.EX
ARRANGE\f[B]:\f[R] COLUMN
ALIGN\f[B]:\f[R] MIDDLE
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]ORDER\f[B]\f[R]
Define the order in which displays are positioned.
The default is simply the order in which the displays are discovered.
This is nondeterministic.
.PP
Array of <\f[I]name\f[R]>
.PP
You are encouraged to explicitly define your order.
e.g.
.IP
.EX
ORDER\f[B]:\f[R]
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aq!\(haDP\-1\(aq
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqMonitor Maker ABC123\(aq
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqHDMI\-1\(aq
.EE
.PP
Regexes are encouraged, allowing for one to easily create generic rules e.g.\ \f[B]\f[CB]\(aq!\(haDP\-\(aq\f[B]\f[R], which will often be sufficient to put external monitors at the top of a column.
.PP
Some displays may be ordered last, by using a \(lqcatchall\(rq regex e.g.
.IP
.EX
ORDER\f[B]:\f[R]
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aq!.*Monitor Maker ABC123.*$\(aq
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aq!.*$\(aq
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqDP\-5\(aq
.EE
.PP
Note that partial matches are not possible in this configuration, and the last displays must be exactly specified.
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]SCALING*\f[B]\f[R]
Control all scaling, including custom \f[B]\f[CB]SCALE\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
0.125 (1/8) is the minimum scaling granularity, see \c
.UR https://github.com/alex-courtis/way-displays/wiki/FAQ#on-scale-and-blurring
FAQ: On Scale And Blurring
.UE \c
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]SCALING\f[B]\f[R]
Boolean, default TRUE
.RS
.PP
Enable scaling, overrides \f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE\f[B]\f[R] and \f[B]\f[CB]SCALE\f[B]\f[R] when disabled.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]SCALE_ROUND_TO\f[B]\f[R]
Number: <\f[B]\f[CB]0.125\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]0.25\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]0.5\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]1\f[B]\f[R]>, default \f[B]\f[CB]0.125\f[B]\f[R]
.RS
.PP
Round scales to nearest, using \f[B]\f[CB]SCALE_ROUND_STRATEGY\f[B]\f[R]
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]SCALE_ROUND_STRATEGY\f[B]\f[R]
Enum <\f[B]\f[CB]NEAREST\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]UP\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]DOWN\f[B]\f[R]>, default \f[B]\f[CB]NEAREST\f[B]\f[R]
.RS
.PP
Applies to \f[B]\f[CB]SCALE_ROUND_TO\f[B]\f[R]
.RE
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# enable all scaling\f[R]
SCALING\f[B]:\f[R] true

\f[I]# round up to the next highest 0.25\f[R]
SCALE_ROUND_STRATEGY\f[B]:\f[R] UP
SCALE_ROUND_TO\f[B]:\f[R] 0.25
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE*\f[B]\f[R]
The default is to scale each display by DPI,
.PP
When disabled a scale 1 will always be used, unless a \f[B]\f[CB]SCALE\f[B]\f[R] has been specified.
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE\f[B]\f[R]
Boolean, default TRUE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE_DPI\f[B]\f[R]
Integer, minimum \f[B]\f[CB]8\f[B]\f[R], default \f[B]\f[CB]96\f[B]\f[R]
.RS
.PP
DPI basis for auto scale calculations.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE_MIN\f[B]\f[R]
Number, default \f[B]\f[CB]1.0\f[B]\f[R]
.RS
.PP
Set minimum scale when auto scaling.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE_MAX\f[B]\f[R]
Number, default \f[B]\f[CB]\-1.0\f[B]\f[R]
.RS
.PP
Set maximum scale when auto scaling, default indicates no maximum.
Ignored if below \f[B]\f[CB]AUTO_SCALE_MIN\f[B]\f[R]
.RE
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# enable auto scaling\f[R]
AUTO_SCALE\f[B]:\f[R] true

\f[I]# using the old fashioned 72 dpi\f[R]
AUTO_SCALE_DPI\f[B]:\f[R] 72

\f[I]# allow half sub\-pixel scaling\f[R]
AUTO_SCALE_MIN\f[B]:\f[R] 0.5

\f[I]# cap your scales to double\f[R]
AUTO_SCALE_MAX\f[B]:\f[R] 2.0
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]SCALE\f[B]\f[R]
Auto scale may be overridden with custom scales for each display.
Does not apply when \f[B]\f[CB]SCALING\f[B]\f[R] FALSE
.PP
Map <\f[I]name\f[R]>: Number
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# ABC123 will be scaled at exactly 1.75 \f[R]
SCALE\f[B]:\f[R]
  \(aqMonitor Maker ABC123\(aq\f[B]:\f[R] 1.75
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]MODE\f[B]\f[R]
Select specific modes for specific displays, overriding auto scaling.
.PP
If the specified mode cannot be found or activated, way\-displays will fall back to the preferred mode, then the highest available resolution / refresh.
.PP
When selecting a mode, \f[CR]way\-displays\f[R] will use the highest refresh within +\-0.5Hz that matches.
There will usually be several refresh rates will match an integegral number of Hz, differing only by a few mHz.
These will be tried in descending order until a working one is found.
.PP
Map <\f[I]name\f[R]>:
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]WIDTH\f[B]\f[R]
Integer, pixels
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]HEIGHT\f[B]\f[R]
Integer, pixels
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]HZ\f[B]\f[R]
Number, refresh rate
.RS
.PP
A refresh rate +\-0.5Hz will match.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]MAX\f[B]\f[R]
Boolean
.RS
.PP
Use the maximum available refresh rate at the maximum resolution.
.RE
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]MAX_PREFERRED_REFRESH\f[B]\f[R]
Boolean
.RS
.PP
Use the highest available refresh for the preferred mode\(cqs resolution.
.RE
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
MODE\f[B]:\f[R]
\f[I]  # highest refresh rate at 1920x1080\f[R]
  HDMI\-A\-1\f[B]:\f[R]
    WIDTH\f[B]:\f[R] 1920
    HEIGHT\f[B]:\f[R] 1080

\f[I]  # 3440x1440 at \(ti144Hz\f[R]
  LG27Q\f[B]:\f[R]
    WIDTH\f[B]:\f[R] 3440
    HEIGHT\f[B]:\f[R] 1440
    HZ\f[B]:\f[R] 144

\f[I]  # maximum available resolution and refresh\f[R]
  M28U\f[B]:\f[R]
    MAX\f[B]:\f[R] TRUE
.EE
.PP
\f[B]WARNING:\f[R] selecting some modes may result in an unusable (blank screen or powered off) monitor.
Try this \f[CR]WLR_DRM_NO_MODIFIERS=1\f[R] \c
.UR https://github.com/alex-courtis/way-displays#unusable-displays-following-mode
workaround
.UE \c
\ if you experience problems.
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]TRANSFORM\f[B]\f[R]
Rotate and mirror the display.
.PP
Map <\f[I]name\f[R]>: Enum <\f[B]\f[CB]90\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]180\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]270\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]FLIPPED\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]FLIPPED\-90\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]FLIPPED\-180\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]FLIPPED\-270\f[B]\f[R]>
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
TRANSFORM\f[B]:\f[R]
\f[I]  # mirror and rotate 180 degrees\f[R]
  LG27Q\f[B]:\f[R] FLIPPED\-180
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]VRR_OFF\f[B]\f[R]
VRR / adaptive sync is enabled by default.
Disable it per display.
.PP
Array of <\f[I]name\f[R]>
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
VRR_OFF\f[B]:\f[R]
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aq!\(haDP\-1\(aq
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqMonitor Maker ABC123\(aq
\f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqHDMI\-1\(aq
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]CALLBACK_CMD\f[B]\f[R]
Sets a \f[CR]/bin/sh\f[R] command to be executed following most events.
.PP
Obeys \f[CR]LOG_THRESHOLD\f[R]
.PP
NOTE: Depending on your compositor this could get executed multiple times when a change happens.
Especially likely on a (dis\-)connect.
.PP
Environment variables: \- \f[CR]${CALLBACK_MSG}\f[R] contains a human readable message \- \f[CR]${CALLBACK_LEVEL}\f[R] one of \f[CR]FATAL\f[R] \f[CR]ERROR\f[R] \f[CR]WARNING\f[R] \f[CR]INFO\f[R]
.PP
String, Default: \f[B]\f[CB]notify\-send \(dqway\-displays ${CALLBACK_LEVEL}\(dq \(dq${CALLBACK_MSG}\(dq\f[B]\f[R]
.PP
A multiline literal YAML string may be used without the \f[CR];\f[R] separators e.g.
.IP
.EX
CALLBACK_CMD\f[B]: \f[R]|
  notify\-send way\-displays \(dqhas done something\(dq
  bell
  notify\-send \(dqway\-displays ${CALLBACK_LEVEL}\(dq \(dq${CALLBACK_MSG}\(dq
.EE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]LAPTOP_LID_MONITOR\f[B]\f[R]
By default, \f[B]\f[CB]libinput(1)\f[B]\f[R] will be used to monitor the laptop lid switch, used by \f[CR]LID\f[R] disabled conditions.
.PP
Boolean, default TRUE
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]LOG_THRESHOLD\f[B]\f[R]
Sets the threshold for log output as well as callbacks.
.PP
Enum <\f[B]\f[CB]FATAL\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]ERROR\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]WARNING\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]INFO\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]DEBUG\f[B]\f[R]>
.SH \f[B]\f[CB]DISABLED\f[B]\f[R]
Display may be disabled always or via an \f[CR]IF\f[R] condition.
.PP
Always disabled:
.PP
Map <\f[I]name\f[R]>: null
.PP
e.g.\ these displays will always be disabled
.IP
.EX
DISABLED\f[B]:\f[R]
\f[B]\-\f[R] eDP\-1
\f[B]\-\f[R] M28U
.EE
.PP
To conditionally disable a display, a list of conditions is supplied via \f[CR]IF\f[R].
One condition must be completely met.
.PP
Map <\f[I]name\f[R]>: \f[B]\f[CB]IF\f[B]\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]\f[CB]IF\f[B]\f[R]
Array of <\f[I]condition\f[R]>
.TP
<\f[I]condition\f[R]>
At least one of:
.RS
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]PLUGGED\f[B]\f[R] Array of <\f[I]name\f[R]> that must all be plugged
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]UNPLUGGED\f[B]\f[R] Array of <\f[I]name\f[R]> that must all be unplugged
.PP
\f[B]\f[CB]LID\f[B]\f[R] Enum <\f[B]\f[CB]OPEN\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]CLOSED\f[B]\f[R]|\f[B]\f[CB]NOT_PRESENT\f[B]\f[R]> when \f[CR]LAPTOP_LID_MONITOR\f[R] is FALSE, \f[CR]NOT_PRESENT\f[R] will evaluate
.RE
.PP
e.g.
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# eDP\-1 will be disabled when:\f[R]
\f[I]#  lid is closed  OR \f[R]
\f[I]#  HDMI\-2 is plugged AND lid is open  OR\f[R]
\f[I]#  DP\-2 is plugged AND HDMI\-1 is unplugged AND lid is open\f[R]
DISABLED\f[B]:\f[R]
  \(aqeDP\-1\(aq\f[B]:\f[R]
    IF\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] LID\f[B]:\f[R] CLOSED
    \f[B]\-\f[R] PLUGGED\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqHDMI\-2\(aq
      LID\f[B]:\f[R] OPEN
    \f[B]\-\f[R] PLUGGED\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqDP\-2\(aq
      UNPLUGGED\f[B]:\f[R]
      \f[B]\-\f[R] \(aqHDMI\-1\(aq
      LID\f[B]:\f[R] OPEN
.EE
.PP
way\-displays will describe the above via logs and callbacks:
.IP
.EX
Disabled:
  eDP\-1
    IF
      lid closed
        OR
      HDMI\-2 plugged AND lid open
        OR
      DP\-2 plugged AND HDMI\-1 unplugged AND lid open
.EE
.PP
The default condition is to disable the laptop display when the lid is closed, and will be automatically populated when creating the default \f[CR]cfg.yaml\f[R] or \f[CR]DISABLED\f[R] is not present:
.IP
.EX
\f[I]# laptop display is disabled when lid is closed\f[R]
DISABLED\f[B]:\f[R]
  \(aq!\(haeDP\-[0\-9]\(aq\f[B]:\f[R]
    IF\f[B]:\f[R]
    \f[B]\-\f[R] LID\f[B]:\f[R] CLOSED
.EE
